    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8767bd4e_e79c26ab",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 7382,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-25T09:48:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I now think that restricting the capabilities from the agent should not, in fact, affect the declared capabilities, so that does mean we need a defensive copy to avoid altering both objects.\n\nI\u0027m not sure what your point is about pointer equality ; the defensive copies are to avoid mutating an object inadvertently by touching another. In fact, I don\u0027t see where you see a defensive copy in handleRegisterNetworkAgent :\n\n final NetworkCapabilities nc \u003d nai.networkCapabilities;\n // Make sure the LinkProperties and NetworkCapabilities reflect what the agent info says.\n processCapabilitiesFromAgent(nai, nc);\n\n...it WILL mutate the NCs stored in the NAI which I thought is already a bit on the risky side, but kept it to avoid more copies.\n\nWe\u0027ve been removing code that directly mutates nai.networkCapabilities but we still have some (e.g. in EVENT_PROBE_STATUS_CHANGED), if we don\u0027t copy it somewhere, we\u0027d mutate the declaredCapabilities member. I\u0027m happy to move that copy in handleRegisterNetworkAgent if you prefer that.",
      "parentUuid": "7a4c39f7_2d74dbb6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7382,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 7382,
        "endChar": 63
      },
